            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Peace with Israel</strong><br>\nIn a historic move, Sadat visited Jerusalem in 1977 to speak before the Israeli Knesset, proposing a peace plan. This visit was the first by an Arab leader to Israel and laid the groundwork for the Camp David Accords. The peace treaty, signed in 1979, ended 30 years of hostilities between Egypt and Israel.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Economic Policies and Infitah</strong><br>\nSadat implemented the Infitah policy to open Egypt's economy to private investment, both foreign and domestic. This shift aimed to modernize the economy and move away from Nasser's socialist policies. However, the results were mixed, with economic benefits accompanied by rising inequality and opposition from those who favored socialist economics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Internal Challenges</strong><br>\nSadat faced significant opposition within Egypt for his peace treaty with Israel. Many Arabs saw it as a betrayal, and Sadat's policies faced criticism for failing to address poverty and unemployment. His leadership was marked by increasing authoritarianism, and he jailed many of his opponents, including religious and leftist groups.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
