          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Foreign Policy and Recognition</strong><br>\nApart from the peace with Israel, Sadat's foreign policy was notable for reorienting Egypt from Soviet to American alignment. His efforts garnered him international respect and recognition, with the Nobel Peace Prize being a prominent accolade. Sadat's leadership helped Egypt regain a central role in Middle Eastern and world politics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Assassination and Legacy</strong><br>\nSadat was assassinated on October 6, 1981, by members of an Islamic extremist military cell during the annual victory parade in Cairo. His death was a dramatic end to a presidency that had profound impacts on the Middle East. Despite the controversy, Sadat's peace efforts have had a lasting influence, and Egypt's relations with Israel remain a pillar of his legacy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Posthumous Recognition</strong><br>\nAfter his death, Sadat was honored both within Egypt and internationally. He is remembered for his bold steps towards peace and for his attempt to modernize Egypt's economy. His leadership style and decisions continue to be studied and debated by historians and political scientists alike.</p>\n"
              },
